An apparatus for controlling delivery of an infusate to a subject comprises a reservoir configured to contain the infusate; a delivery mechanism operatively coupled to the reservoir; a user interface; a processor; and a non-transitory memory storing instructions executable by the processor.

The processor is configured to receive, through the user interface, a bolus-delivery command specifying a total bolus quantity and an allocation of the total bolus quantity between (i) an immediate portion and (ii) an extended portion. In response to the bolus-delivery command, the processor controls the delivery mechanism to deliver the immediate portion over a first delivery interval and to deliver the extended portion over a user-specified or system-determined extension interval. The extended portion may be delivered at a substantially uniform rate, according to a plurality of discrete delivery pulses, or in accordance with a time-varying delivery profile.

The processor is further configured to receive a temporary-rate command specifying a temporary delivery rate, or a percentage adjustment relative to a programmed baseline rate, and a defined duration for application of the temporary delivery rate. During the defined duration, the processor controls the delivery mechanism according to the temporary delivery rate and, upon expiration or termination of the defined duration, automatically resumes the programmed baseline rate or another scheduled delivery profile.

The processor may coordinate the immediate portion, the extended portion, the temporary delivery rate, and the baseline rate by calculating an aggregate commanded delivery for each control interval. Where the aggregate commanded delivery exceeds a predetermined delivery limit, reservoir limit, or safety constraint, the processor may suspend, defer, reduce, apportion, or cancel at least one delivery component and generate an audible, visual, or tactile notification. The apparatus may additionally maintain a delivery history identifying commanded, delivered, remaining, interrupted, and canceled quantities.

A corresponding method comprises:

1. receiving a bolus request identifying a total bolus quantity;
2. receiving an allocation parameter defining an immediate portion and an extended portion of the total bolus quantity;
3. delivering the immediate portion during a first interval;
4. delivering the extended portion during an extension interval;
5. receiving a temporary-rate parameter and a duration parameter;
6. controlling delivery at the temporary rate for the defined duration;
7. determining, during any temporal overlap, a combined delivery command based on the extended portion, the temporary rate, and any programmed baseline delivery;
8. applying one or more delivery constraints to the combined delivery command; and
9. automatically restoring the programmed baseline delivery upon expiration, cancellation, or completion of the temporary-rate period.

The method may further include permitting modification, suspension, or cancellation of an undelivered part of the extended portion or temporary-rate period; calculating the quantity actually delivered; storing event and delivery records; detecting occlusion, depletion, actuator error, or communication failure; and issuing an alarm or entering a safe operating state in response thereto.
